              "Step 1: Draw pupils’ attention to the text and read it together as a class. Check comprehension.Step 2: Draw pupils’ attention to the four statements below the text and four boxes to tick True or False. Have pupils pay attention to the sentences about the rooms in the school (their locations, the directions to the rooms). Give pupils time to tick the correct boxes. Ask where in the text pupils can find the information they need to decide whether the statements are true or false. For example, for Statement 1, explain that pupils need to find the sentence Our school has a computer room and a library. in the text, then tick the box True near the statement.Step 3: Tell pupils to swap books with a partner, then check answers together as a class. Write the correct answers on the board.Step 4: If time allows, ask pupils to read the text aloud in front of the class. Have pupils read the sentences in the reading text that help them find the correct answers. For example, You go upstairs, and it is on the left.Extension: Have pupils write sentences to compare the location of and direction to the rooms in the reading and the rooms in their school. For example: Our computer room is on the ground floor, but their computer room is on the second floor.",
              "- Pupils pay attention to the text and read it as a class.- Pupils read the four statements below the text, and find the information in the text to decide the  statements are True or False.- Pupils swap books with a partner, then check answers as a class.- Pupils read the text aloud in front of the class.- Pupils read the sentences in the text that help them find the answers.- Pupils write the sentences about the same rooms in their school, giving the location and direction to those rooms."
